# Auto detect text files and perform LF normalization
* text=auto

# Execution-sensitive scripts must be LF (run in bash/CI). Covers the relocated tools/ kits.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The gate leg manifest is runtime-read by run-gates.sh (a stray CR would corrupt leg names/argv).
tools/gate-legs.json text eol=lf

# Git hooks are extensionless (the *.sh rule above misses them); a CRLF hook won't execute.
.githooks/pre-commit text eol=lf
.githooks/pre-push text eol=lf

# memory-tree determinism (this repo dogfoods the kit). THREE gates read these bytes, and all three
# are byte- or char-exact:
#   check 9  byte-compares the generated build index against a fresh LF render — an unpinned
#            generated file on a Windows checkout differs on EVERY line, so it reds on a file nobody
#            touched and goes green only right after a render;
#   check 6  caps index files at 20 KB;
#   check 7  caps an index ENTRY at 300 chars.
# A CRLF checkout adds one byte to every LINE, so checks 6 and 7 measure something different per
# platform: the same corpus passes on Linux and fails on Windows. Measured — DECISIONS.md at
# 20466 B (LF) reported 20559 B against the 20480 B cap in the primary tree and blocked a push,
# while the worktree that wrote it read green. AGENTS.md already states LF for "the memory-tree data
# files"; this is that claim made true.
#
# Pinned by EXTENSION rather than by listing today's index files, because the index set is DERIVED
# (`check-memory-hygiene.sh --print-index-set`): a new backlog shard, guide, or build STATUS.md joins
# it with no edit here. A pin that must be remembered is the pin that gets forgotten.
memory/**/*.md text eol=lf
memory/**/*.toml text eol=lf
memory/**/*.txt text eol=lf

# The two AUTHORED indexes every node appends to. `merge=rows` routes a conflict through the
# row-keyed driver (tools/memory-tree/merge-rows.py), which merges by record id: an append-collision
# between two nodes auto-resolves with both rows present and no duplicate id. Deliberately NOT
# `merge=union` — union never loses an id but introduced a duplicate in 147 of 151 historical
# DECISIONS.md conflicts upstream, and these files hold zero duplicate ids.
# No `eol=lf` pin here on purpose: `* text=auto` above already normalises the committed blobs, the
# driver is newline-preserving by construction, and a pin would rewrite every node's worktree bytes
# on its next checkout for nothing. A node with no `merge.rows.driver` configured falls back to git's
# built-in three-way text merge with a warning — the pre-change behaviour, which is why the attribute
# and the per-node config can land in one commit. `bash tools/check-wiring.sh --fix` configures it.
memory/DECISIONS.md merge=rows
memory/backlog/*.md merge=rows

# The governance template is under a STRICT byte gate — pin it (+ companions) to LF so a CRLF
# checkout can't inflate the count past the limit. (The gate also normalizes, belt-and-suspenders.)
parallel-coding-governance*.md text eol=lf

# The rendered recall Skill is compared to a fresh render with plain `diff` by
# `adopt-memory-recall.sh --check`. The renderer always emits LF, so without this pin a Windows
# checkout turns the file CRLF and the leg reds on EVERY line — while passing whenever the working
# copy happens to have just been rendered. That is green-by-accident, not a gate (§7).
.claude/skills/memory-recall/SKILL.md text eol=lf

# The rendered drift-audit Skill, same reason. Belt-and-braces rather than load-bearing here:
# `adopt-drift-audit.sh --check` strips CR on BOTH sides before diffing, so it cannot red on a CRLF
# checkout. The pin keeps the committed bytes deterministic anyway, and stops a future maintainer who
# drops that `tr -d '\r'` from reintroducing memory-recall's green-by-accident failure.
.claude/skills/drift-audit/SKILL.md text eol=lf

# codebase-map determinism (this repo dogfoods the kit). The gate's freshness leg BYTE-COMPARES the
# generated artifacts against a fresh LF render, so an unpinned generated file on a Windows checkout
# differs on EVERY line — the same green-only-right-after-a-render failure the memory-tree pins above
# exist for. The conf is pinned because it is SOURCED BY BASH: a CRLF checkout leaves stray carriage
# returns in every value on Linux.
.codebase-map.conf text eol=lf
memory/map/generated/* text eol=lf
memory/map/baseline.toml text eol=lf
memory/map/affordance-exempt.toml text eol=lf
